    {
      "question": "ORACLE -- can articulation be recovered given the true acoustic map?",
      "ground_truth": {
        "latent trajectories u(t)": "known exactly"
      },
      "estimate": {
        "noise 0.01": 0.00032,
        "noise 0.1": 0.00321,
        "noise 0.3": 0.00963,
        "noise 1.0": 0.0321
      },
      "error": 0.003209402713139786,
      "status": "IDENTIFIABLE",
      "control": "noise 0.01 gives 3.21e-04, so the residual is noise, not bias",
      "notes": "with Phi known, inversion is essentially exact and degrades only as fast as the noise on the transition-averaged trajectory. The temporal inverse problem is easy; everything hard in this pipeline lives in the acoustic map.",
      "error_label": "relative rmse at noise 0.1"
    },
    {
      "question": "ORACLE-INPUT -- can DPhi be estimated by probing the latent state?",
      "ground_truth": {
        "DPhi(u)": "analytic, verified against finite differences to 1e-9"
      },
      "estimate": {
        "best relative error": 8.960021766428902e-06,
        "at (noise, n_repeat)": "(0.01, 1000)"
      },
      "error": 8.960021766428902e-06,
      "status": "IDENTIFIABLE",
      "control": "the saturating map, run through the identical sweep: its optimum sits at delta = 0.03, interior to the swept range",
      "notes": "on the quadratic map the best step is delta = 3, an edge optimum -- and it should be an edge one: central differences truncate at O(delta^2 * third derivative) and a quadratic map has no third derivative, so the estimator is unbiased at every step and error falls as sigma/(delta sqrt(n)). The bias/variance trade-off only becomes visible on the saturating map, which is why both are swept. Reading the monotone curve as a trade-off would have been a comfortable and wrong conclusion.",
      "error_label": "relative Frobenius error"
    },
    {
      "question": "BLIND -- can DPhi be estimated from acoustics alone?",
      "ground_truth": {
        "column space of DPhi": "2-dimensional inside R^3"
